<!--
  Name:     F100-PW-220
  File:     F100-PW-220.xml
  Author:   Richard Harrison (rjh@zaretto.com)
  Date:     13 December 2014

  Pratt & Whitney F100-PW-220 afterburning low-bypass turbofan engine.
  Powers the F-15C/D Eagle (two engines per aircraft).

  Published specifications (F100-PW-220):
    Military (intermediate) thrust:  14,590 lbf (64.9 kN)
    Maximum (afterburning) thrust:   23,770 lbf (105.7 kN)
    Bypass ratio:                    0.36:1
    Overall pressure ratio:          25:1
    TSFC (military):                 0.73 lb/(lbf.h)
    TSFC (max afterburner):          1.94 lb/(lbf.h)
    Mass flow:                       228 lb/s
    Dry weight:                      3,234 lb
    Length:                          191 in
    Max diameter:                    46.5 in

  Modelling approach
  ==================
  JSBSim FGTurbine thrust formulas (from FGTurbine.cpp):
    Idle:       thrust_idle = milthrust_scalar * IdleTable(mach, alt)
    Military:   thrust = thrust_idle + (milthrust_scalar - thrust_idle) * MilTable(mach, alt)
    Afterburner: thrust = maxthrust_scalar * AugTable(mach, alt)

  The MilThrust and AugThrust tables are normalised so that the Mach 0 / sea-level
  entry produces the published rated static thrust when combined with the scalar
  values above. i.e. MilTable(0,0) = 1.0 and AugTable(0,0) = 1.0.

  Thrust variation with Mach and altitude is modelled using corrected-thrust
  thermodynamic principles for a low-bypass-ratio turbofan:

    1. Total pressure at the engine face:
         Pt2 = P_ambient * (1 + 0.2*M^2)^3.5 * eta_inlet(M)
       where eta_inlet is the F-15 variable-geometry inlet pressure recovery.

    2. Total temperature at the engine face:
         Tt2 = T_ambient * (1 + 0.2*M^2)

    3. Military thrust scales as corrected thrust:
         T_mil / T_mil_SLS ~ Pt2 / sqrt(Tt2)
       with engine limiting (compressor surge margin and turbine temperature
       limits) applied as a soft cap when corrected thrust exceeds ~1.45.
       Temperature derating reduces the limit at high inlet temperatures
       (high Mach / low altitude) to model TIT-limited operation.

    4. Afterburner thrust scales similarly but is less temperature-sensitive
       (the afterburner adds a fixed enthalpy rise regardless of inlet temp):
         T_aug / T_aug_SLS ~ Pt2 / Tt2^0.35
       with a higher soft cap (~1.55) reflecting the AB's ability to extract
       more energy from high-ram conditions.

    5. The F-15 variable-geometry inlet provides excellent pressure recovery
       subsonically (eta=1.0) with gradual degradation supersonically,
       modelled as: eta = 1.0 - 0.075*(M-1.0)^1.35 for M > 1.0

  The resulting AB/Mil thrust ratio increases smoothly from ~1.63 at static
  to ~1.85 at Mach 2.5, which is consistent with published data for low-BPR
  afterburning turbofans.

  Validation
  ==========
  - Sea level static: Mil = 14,590 lbs, AB = 23,770 lbs (matches published specs)
  - T/W ratio at SL static AB, 2 engines, 36,000 lbs clean weight: 1.32 (correct)
  - Thrust at M1.5/40,000 ft closely matches previous model which was tuned
    against TO-1F-15A-1 Appendix B figure B9-21 (level flight acceleration,
    gross weight 40,000 lbs, page B9-26)
  - Smooth monotonic thrust increase with Mach (no anomalous subsonic spikes)
  - Altitude lapse consistent with standard atmosphere pressure ratios

  History
  =======
  2014-12-13  RJH  Initial version based on NASA TM-86042 tables normalised
  2019-10-24  RJH  Tuned Mil and Max power using TO-1F-15A-1 APP B PERF DATA
                   F100-PW-220; figure B9-21 Level Flight Acceleration GW 40,000lbs
  2026-03-06  RJH  Complete rework of thrust tables:
                   - Normalised tables so M0/SL produces rated static thrust
                     (previous tables produced only 70-77% of rated thrust)
                   - Replaced empirical curve-fit with corrected-thrust
                     thermodynamic model including engine limiting
                   - Fixed unrealistic M0.25 thrust spike (was 185% of rated)
                   - Gave AugThrust its own distinct table shape (previously
                     Mil and Aug tables had inconsistent shapes giving erratic
                     AB/Mil ratios from 1.5x to 2.9x; now smooth 1.63-1.85x)
                   - Fixed bypass ratio from 0.63 to 0.36
                   - Maintained calibration at M1.5/40kft against flight manual

  References
  ==========
